    "strategy": {
      "summary": "Mapletree Industrial Trust's (MIT) decarbonization strategy for 2023 is fundamentally aligned with Mapletree Group's 'Net Zero by 2050' roadmap, implementing a structured approach focused on energy efficiency and renewable energy adoption. The company has established specific, measurable targets across its portfolio, including successfully meeting its FY22/23 target of reducing building electricity intensity by 3.0% for properties in Singapore compared to the FY21/22 base year. This translated into actual performance improvements of 10.9% reduction in average building electricity intensity and 11.9% reduction in Scope 2 GHG emissions intensity for Singapore properties from FY21/22. MIT's long-term strategy includes reducing building electricity intensity by 15% and Scope 2 GHG emissions intensity by 17% by FY29/30, while expanding solar energy generating capacity across its portfolio to 10,000 kWp.\n\nTo operationalize this strategy, MIT has formalized four key sustainability policies that provide comprehensive frameworks for decarbonization efforts. These include the Group Renewable Energy Policy outlining operational energy hierarchy and renewable procurement guidelines, the Group Sustainable Development Policy covering sustainable practices for greenfield and brownfield developments, the Group Sustainable Investment Policy for sustainability assessments during due diligence, and the Group Sustainable Operations Policy for sustainable asset management. The company has also conducted climate risk assessments using Net Zero (RCP 2.6) and Business-as-usual (RCP 8.5) scenarios to identify relevant climate-related risks and opportunities, including transition risks like increased carbon pricing and physical risks such as extreme weather events.\n\nSupporting their decarbonization journey, MIT has achieved significant green building certification coverage, with 42% of its total portfolio (by AUM) holding sustainability building certifications. This includes BCA Green Mark Platinum and GoldPlus awards for specific properties, with 16 properties certified under BCA Green Mark and LEED, and 5 properties with Energy Star ratings. The company's approach integrates environmental considerations into all aspects of real estate operations, from development and investment to ongoing operations, reflecting their commitment to the triple bottom line principle that broadens business focus beyond financial returns to incorporate social and environmental considerations.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across Sustainability Report pages. Strategy focuses primarily on operational emissions with specific near-term and long-term targets. Implementation details provided for energy efficiency and renewable energy initiatives. Climate risk assessment methodology included. Strategy is well-structured with supporting policies and actual performance metrics.",
      "status": null
    },
